{"name":"Bow Tie","hex":"#be2633","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bow tie","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of bowtie."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bow_tie"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bow tie","blocks":[{"text":"A bow tie or dicky bow is a type of neckwear, distinguishable from a necktie because it does not drape down the shirt placket, but is tied just underneath a collar. A modern bow tie is tied using a common shoelace knot, which is also called the bow knot for that reason. It consists of a ribbon of fabric tied around the collar of a shirt in a symmetrical manner so that the two opposite ends form loops."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Bow-tie-colour-isolated.jpg/330px-Bow-tie-colour-isolated.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bow_tie"},{"title":"Bow tie (biology)","blocks":[{"text":"In the biological sciences, the term bow tie is a concept that tries to grasp the essence of some operational and functional structures observed in biological organisms and other kinds of complex and self-organizing systems. In general, bow tie architectures refer to ordered and recurrent structures that often underlie complex technological or biological systems, and that are capable of conferring them a balance among efficiency, robustness and evolvability. In other words, bow ties are able to take into account a great diversity of inputs, showing a much smaller diversity in the protocols and processes able to elaborate these inputs, and finally an extremely heterogeneous diversity of outputs. These architectures thus manage a wide range of inputs through a core (knot) constituted by a limited number of elements. In such structures, inputs are conveyed into a sort of funnel, towards a \"synthesis\" core, where they can be duly organized, processed and managed by means of protocols, and from where, in turn, a variety of outputs, or responses, is propagated."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/General_bowtie_architecture.png/330px-General_bowtie_architecture.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bow_tie_(biology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Ziehrer.jpg/250px-Ziehrer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b4/Ziehrer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Ziehrer.jpg/250px-Ziehrer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Karl Michael Ziehrer wearing a 19th-century style bow tie","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZiehrer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/HowToTieBowtie_VersionA.png/500px-HowToTieBowtie_VersionA.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/HowToTieBowtie_VersionA.png/500px-HowToTieBowtie_VersionA.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Instructions on a common way to tie a bow tie","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHowToTieBowtie_VersionA.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/2_Silk_Bow_Ties.JPG/120px-2_Silk_Bow_Ties.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/2_Silk_Bow_Ties.JPG/250px-2_Silk_Bow_Ties.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/2_Silk_Bow_Ties.JPG/120px-2_Silk_Bow_Ties.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&